Product reconfiguration enables users to adapt products to changing needs and contexts throughout their lifecycle, extending utility and reducing the need for replacement. By designing modular architectures that allow components to be rearranged, upgraded, or substituted, designers create more flexible solutions that can accommodate evolving requirements without disposal. This approach is particularly valuable in rapidly changing contexts where user needs are uncertain or where single-use fixed designs lead to premature obsolescence.

Reconfigurable design requires balancing modularity with simplicity, ensuring that the ability to reconfigure does not compromise usability or increase complexity for typical users. Interface standardization, intuitive connection systems, and clear guidance on reconfiguration options become essential. This strategy connects directly to service-oriented thinking, where products become platforms for value delivery rather than static objects, supporting both individual customization and broader circular business models.